WEBVTT

00:00:03.120 --> 00:00:03.780
RAVISH PATEL: Hi, everyone.

00:00:03.780 --> 00:00:07.200
My name is Ravish and I
work at PingCAP on TiDB

00:00:07.200 --> 00:00:08.850
as a solutions engineer.

00:00:08.850 --> 00:00:12.270
Over the next 20 minutes or
so, I'm going to show you how

00:00:12.270 --> 00:00:17.370
to build agent memory for your
AI agents using just SQL.

00:00:17.370 --> 00:00:20.070
There's also a demo halfway
through, so hopefully,

00:00:20.070 --> 00:00:22.960
you stick around, and
let's get started.

00:00:25.980 --> 00:00:28.620
Before we get into TiDB,
real quick, I want to talk

00:00:28.620 --> 00:00:31.830
about why agents are different.

00:00:31.830 --> 00:00:34.740
It's because they hit a database
in completely different ways

00:00:34.740 --> 00:00:37.640
than the apps you've
worked on before.

00:00:37.640 --> 00:00:39.390
There are three big things.

00:00:39.390 --> 00:00:42.510
First, agent workloads
are bursty, right?

00:00:42.510 --> 00:00:45.600
One agent might be running
out flat for 10 seconds,

00:00:45.600 --> 00:00:47.880
and then the other one
might be sitting idle

00:00:47.880 --> 00:00:52.050
for two hours doing nothing, and
your database has to handle both

00:00:52.050 --> 00:00:54.840
without costing you
a lot of money.

00:00:54.840 --> 00:00:58.230
Second is you're dealing
with massive concurrency.

00:00:58.230 --> 00:01:02.130
It's not an app with a
thousand users, but instead,

00:01:02.130 --> 00:01:04.980
you're running millions of
agents at the same time

00:01:04.980 --> 00:01:08.340
and each with their own state.

00:01:08.340 --> 00:01:11.700
Third is constant
context recall.

00:01:11.700 --> 00:01:13.920
Basically, every step
the agent takes,

00:01:13.920 --> 00:01:16.320
it has to remember
what just happened.

00:01:16.320 --> 00:01:20.250
Otherwise, the whole
thing will fall apart.

00:01:20.250 --> 00:01:23.520
Those are the three things
you can't really skip.

00:01:23.520 --> 00:01:27.760
Let me quickly show you what
most teams end up doing today.

00:01:32.160 --> 00:01:33.600
Here's the situation.

00:01:33.600 --> 00:01:36.960
Let's say an agent finishes
a chat with the user.

00:01:36.960 --> 00:01:38.640
The user comes back tomorrow,

00:01:38.640 --> 00:01:41.970
and the agent has no
clue who the user is.

00:01:41.970 --> 00:01:45.150
It's like the conversation
never happened.

00:01:45.150 --> 00:01:47.640
To fix that, what most teams end

00:01:47.640 --> 00:01:51.750
up doing is they run three
different databases plus a bunch

00:01:51.750 --> 00:01:56.070
of ETL pipelines, and
that's their memory layer.

00:01:56.070 --> 00:01:59.370
You've got your regular database
for storing chat history,

00:01:59.370 --> 00:02:02.130
user accounts, that
kind of stuff,

00:02:02.130 --> 00:02:04.890
and that's the source of truth.

00:02:04.890 --> 00:02:07.980
Then on top of that, you
have a vector database

00:02:07.980 --> 00:02:10.890
for semantic search so
you can store embeddings

00:02:10.890 --> 00:02:13.530
of whatever the user said.

00:02:13.530 --> 00:02:16.320
On top of that, you
have a search engine

00:02:16.320 --> 00:02:19.860
because vectors are
bad at exact matches.

00:02:19.860 --> 00:02:23.130
If the user said a word
like, let's say, "Tokyo,"

00:02:23.130 --> 00:02:24.750
you want a keyword index

00:02:24.750 --> 00:02:28.350
to actually find
that word, "Tokyo."

00:02:28.350 --> 00:02:30.960
Then there's the fourth piece
nobody really talks about,

00:02:30.960 --> 00:02:32.490
which is the glue layer.

00:02:32.490 --> 00:02:36.600
That's your ETL jobs, your
message buses, the client jobs,

00:02:36.600 --> 00:02:40.530
something to keep all three
of these things in sync,

00:02:40.530 --> 00:02:45.180
and your team has to write
and maintain that code.

00:02:45.180 --> 00:02:48.450
So now, you've got
three databases to run,

00:02:48.450 --> 00:02:50.910
three separate builds
from three databases,

00:02:50.910 --> 00:02:54.240
and three things that can break.

00:02:54.240 --> 00:02:56.850
On top of that, your
data is never really

00:02:56.850 --> 00:03:00.040
in sync, so it's just a lot.

00:03:03.210 --> 00:03:07.020
Now, look, if you're building
just a regular application,

00:03:07.020 --> 00:03:09.210
you can kind of sort
of live with this.

00:03:09.210 --> 00:03:12.270
It's annoying, but I guess
you can deal with it.

00:03:12.270 --> 00:03:15.750
However, with agents, though,
this stack breaks in a bunch

00:03:15.750 --> 00:03:17.850
of ways, and here's why.

00:03:17.850 --> 00:03:20.190
The first one is stale data.

00:03:20.190 --> 00:03:23.640
Your vector index is always a
few seconds behind your main

00:03:23.640 --> 00:03:29.190
database, so the agent ends up
reading old data and old facts.

00:03:29.190 --> 00:03:31.680
Let's say a user updated
their address an hour ago,

00:03:31.680 --> 00:03:34.860
but the agent is still
using the old one.

00:03:34.860 --> 00:03:37.890
Then you've got the
read-after-write problem.

00:03:37.890 --> 00:03:41.520
Say your agent just placed an
order, and two seconds later,

00:03:41.520 --> 00:03:44.670
the user asks, "What is
the status of my order?"

00:03:44.670 --> 00:03:47.880
and the agent says, "I
don't see any orders."

00:03:47.880 --> 00:03:53.010
The write just happened, but the
read side hasn't caught up yet.

00:03:53.010 --> 00:03:55.860
Then the third one
is partial writes.

00:03:55.860 --> 00:03:58.320
Agent, let's say, does
a multi-step thing,

00:03:58.320 --> 00:04:00.300
like processing a refund.

00:04:00.300 --> 00:04:03.930
It deducts the balance,
logs the refund event,

00:04:03.930 --> 00:04:07.470
triggers some sort of an email.

00:04:07.470 --> 00:04:10.830
Now, midway through,
the email service dies

00:04:10.830 --> 00:04:14.400
so the balance shows that
the refund was applied.

00:04:14.400 --> 00:04:16.890
The log says that it happened,

00:04:16.890 --> 00:04:19.230
but the user never
got the email.

00:04:19.230 --> 00:04:22.710
Then the user calls the support,
and support sees the logs

00:04:22.710 --> 00:04:24.330
and says it looks fine,

00:04:24.330 --> 00:04:28.260
so you're stuck explaining
a ghost refund.

00:04:28.260 --> 00:04:32.280
The last one is a connection
fan out, so for example,

00:04:32.280 --> 00:04:36.510
let's say you have 10,000 agents
times the three systems is

00:04:36.510 --> 00:04:41.790
basically 30,000 connections you
need to maintain and manage.

00:04:41.790 --> 00:04:44.610
Your connection pools
can basically keep

00:04:44.610 --> 00:04:47.130
up with those connections,
so your latency goes

00:04:47.130 --> 00:04:50.100
up when the traffic
spikes, and you spend half

00:04:50.100 --> 00:04:52.770
of your time managing
connections instead

00:04:52.770 --> 00:04:54.880
of building features.

00:04:56.070 --> 00:05:00.630
Look, this is all not just
theory or hypothetical.

00:05:00.630 --> 00:05:04.530
This stuff actually happens in
production, and you only run

00:05:04.530 --> 00:05:06.570
into it once you're at scale,

00:05:06.570 --> 00:05:10.210
which is the worst possible
time for it to happen.

00:05:13.620 --> 00:05:15.990
That's where TiDB
comes into play.

00:05:15.990 --> 00:05:18.480
Real quick, in case you
haven't heard of TiDB before,

00:05:18.480 --> 00:05:20.310
here's a quick summary.

00:05:20.310 --> 00:05:22.980
So first, it is a
distributed SQL database,

00:05:22.980 --> 00:05:25.860
so it can scale
horizontally across nodes,

00:05:25.860 --> 00:05:28.140
so no sharding, no rewrites.

00:05:28.140 --> 00:05:32.310
You just add nodes and
capacity whenever you need it.

00:05:32.310 --> 00:05:34.560
Second, it is MySQL compatible.

00:05:34.560 --> 00:05:38.100
Any drivers you have, any
ORM, any tools you're using

00:05:38.100 --> 00:05:42.330
with MySQL, it works
with TiDB as well.

00:05:42.330 --> 00:05:47.310
Third is it has an HTAP and an
AI engine, so transactions,

00:05:47.310 --> 00:05:49.470
analytics, vector search,

00:05:49.470 --> 00:05:54.510
and full-text search all
live in the same database.

00:05:54.510 --> 00:05:58.410
Fourth, it's used in production
by many of our customers

00:05:58.410 --> 00:06:03.840
like Manus, Pinterest,
Dify, and a lot of others.

00:06:03.840 --> 00:06:07.290
That's basically a quick
overview of what TiDB is.

00:06:07.290 --> 00:06:10.540
Now let's go ahead and get
into the capabilities.

00:06:13.680 --> 00:06:14.220
All right.

00:06:14.220 --> 00:06:18.000
Here's what TiDB
gives your agents.

00:06:18.000 --> 00:06:21.270
The first three rows fix
the three data problems

00:06:21.270 --> 00:06:24.990
that we just talked about,
and the last two are extras

00:06:24.990 --> 00:06:27.840
that kind of just
make your life easier

00:06:27.840 --> 00:06:29.880
when you're building agents.

00:06:29.880 --> 00:06:33.120
The first one is agent
state and chat history.

00:06:33.120 --> 00:06:35.220
That's just normal SQL.

00:06:35.220 --> 00:06:37.320
Next up, we have
semantic recall,

00:06:37.320 --> 00:06:41.580
which is a vector column
with an HLSW index.

00:06:41.580 --> 00:06:45.390
Next is keyword search,
which is a full-text index

00:06:45.390 --> 00:06:49.800
with a multilingual parser, so
it can handle English, Spanish,

00:06:49.800 --> 00:06:52.950
Japanese, whatever
you throw at it.

00:06:52.950 --> 00:06:56.460
Then we have embeddings
on insert, which is nice.

00:06:56.460 --> 00:07:01.020
There's actually a SQL function
called, "embed underscore text,"

00:07:01.020 --> 00:07:03.300
which basically you
can give it some text

00:07:03.300 --> 00:07:05.160
and it will call
the embedding model

00:07:05.160 --> 00:07:09.270
and it will store the vector
for you in the table.

00:07:09.270 --> 00:07:11.580
The last one is
hybrid retrieval,

00:07:11.580 --> 00:07:14.820
so you can run a vector
search and on top of that,

00:07:14.820 --> 00:07:18.240
you can run a keyword search
and then you can combine them

00:07:18.240 --> 00:07:21.220
with some sort of
ranking algorithm.

00:07:22.230 --> 00:07:24.690
We're going to see all
of this during the demo

00:07:24.690 --> 00:07:26.620
in just a second.

00:07:29.130 --> 00:07:33.630
Now, just one more slide and
then we'll get into the demo.

00:07:33.630 --> 00:07:35.670
This goes back to what
I said at the start

00:07:35.670 --> 00:07:39.240
about agents having different
needs from a database.

00:07:39.240 --> 00:07:43.530
The first thing you get from
TiDB is you get scale to zero.

00:07:43.530 --> 00:07:45.780
If you have idle agents,

00:07:45.780 --> 00:07:48.090
they literally don't
cost you anything.

00:07:48.090 --> 00:07:51.420
You're only paying for the
requests you actually run

00:07:51.420 --> 00:07:57.030
so when your traffic is bursty,
your bill is bursty too.

00:07:57.030 --> 00:07:59.280
Then you get database branching.

00:07:59.280 --> 00:08:02.520
You can spin up an isolated
database per agent

00:08:02.520 --> 00:08:04.860
in just a few milliseconds.

00:08:04.860 --> 00:08:07.830
Every agent gets
its own workspace,

00:08:07.830 --> 00:08:11.280
totally separated
from everyone else.

00:08:11.280 --> 00:08:13.530
Third is resource control

00:08:13.530 --> 00:08:17.070
so you can cap how much
each workload uses,

00:08:17.070 --> 00:08:20.160
so if one agent
goes off the rail,

00:08:20.160 --> 00:08:23.520
it doesn't take down
everything else.

00:08:23.520 --> 00:08:26.100
The last one is fast scale out.

00:08:26.100 --> 00:08:29.400
Compute and storage
are separated in TiDB,

00:08:29.400 --> 00:08:33.060
so when the traffic spikes,
the database can add capacity

00:08:33.060 --> 00:08:36.280
in seconds instead of minutes.

00:08:37.760 --> 00:08:39.350
Okay, that's enough slides.

00:08:39.350 --> 00:08:43.160
Let me show you in the database.

00:08:43.160 --> 00:08:47.520
Okay. In Demo 1, we're going
to create the memory table.

00:08:50.820 --> 00:08:54.990
Okay. Here we are in the SQL
editor on a free TiDB cluster,

00:08:54.990 --> 00:08:58.410
and I'm going to run the
whole demo on just one table.

00:08:58.410 --> 00:09:01.770
So up top, you see normal
columns like a user ID,

00:09:01.770 --> 00:09:06.270
just a regular ID,
content, a timestamp.

00:09:06.270 --> 00:09:09.440
The interesting one, however,
is the embedding column.

00:09:09.440 --> 00:09:13.280
It's a vector with
1,536 dimensions,

00:09:13.280 --> 00:09:15.710
and it is a generated column.

00:09:15.710 --> 00:09:18.420
Every time a row gets inserted,

00:09:18.420 --> 00:09:21.720
TiDB calls the function
called embed underscore text,

00:09:21.720 --> 00:09:25.200
which is pointed at my
Azure Open API deployment,

00:09:25.200 --> 00:09:28.260
and it generates the
embedding and stores it,

00:09:28.260 --> 00:09:30.810
no Python, no pipeline.

00:09:30.810 --> 00:09:33.510
Plus, at the end, you also
see there's a vector index

00:09:33.510 --> 00:09:37.770
for semantic search and a full
text index for keyword search,

00:09:37.770 --> 00:09:39.510
both on the same table.

00:09:39.510 --> 00:09:42.970
As you can see, the
table is created.

00:09:45.780 --> 00:09:48.960
The step #2 for the demo
is inserting memories

00:09:48.960 --> 00:09:51.040
and seeing the data.

00:09:55.320 --> 00:09:59.520
Now, I'm going to drop the
five memories for user ID 42.

00:09:59.520 --> 00:10:03.000
As you can see, they are
plain English strings,

00:10:03.000 --> 00:10:05.490
no embedding code in
my script anywhere,

00:10:05.490 --> 00:10:09.420
but TiDB is calling the Azure
Open API in the background

00:10:09.420 --> 00:10:12.420
to generate the embeddings.

00:10:12.420 --> 00:10:16.230
There they are, so you see
five rows for "Jazz," "Vinyl,"

00:10:16.230 --> 00:10:20.250
"Peanut Allergy," "Tokyo
Flight," "Email Preference,"

00:10:20.250 --> 00:10:23.400
and yeah, that's our data
set that we're going to use

00:10:23.400 --> 00:10:25.840
for the rest of the demo.

00:10:31.680 --> 00:10:35.620
Step #3 is going to
be semantic search.

00:10:38.580 --> 00:10:41.280
So here, the agent is
asking, let's say,

00:10:41.280 --> 00:10:43.860
what does the user
like to listen to?

00:10:43.860 --> 00:10:46.800
So notice, my question doesn't
share a single keyword

00:10:46.800 --> 00:10:48.840
with anything in the database.

00:10:48.840 --> 00:10:54.270
There's no listen, no like,
just Jazz and Miles Davis,

00:10:54.270 --> 00:10:58.500
but as you can see, the
jazz row comes back first,

00:10:58.500 --> 00:11:02.310
and you can see the
distance is 0.49.

00:11:02.310 --> 00:11:05.040
That's the closest
batch by meaning,

00:11:05.040 --> 00:11:08.640
so TiDB took my English
question, embedded it,

00:11:08.640 --> 00:11:14.230
and ranked the rows by
similarity, all in one SQL call.

00:11:16.740 --> 00:11:19.750
Step #4 is keyword search.

00:11:22.980 --> 00:11:27.210
Now, sometimes you want exact
word matching instead, you know,

00:11:27.210 --> 00:11:30.240
so vectors are kind
of bad at that.

00:11:30.240 --> 00:11:32.610
Here, we're using a full
text search to check

00:11:32.610 --> 00:11:37.950
if the user mentioned a city,
and once we execute the query,

00:11:37.950 --> 00:11:40.800
you can see there will
be a row, the Tokyo row,

00:11:40.800 --> 00:11:42.480
which is the exact match.

00:11:42.480 --> 00:11:46.650
Then you can also see
the score of 1.34.

00:11:46.650 --> 00:11:51.210
The big thing here is this is
the same table I just did vector

00:11:51.210 --> 00:11:55.840
search on, no separate engine,
to keep everything in sync.

00:11:57.930 --> 00:12:01.030
Step #5 is hybrid search.

00:12:04.110 --> 00:12:06.300
So, okay, I guess this
one really matters,

00:12:06.300 --> 00:12:10.320
so real hybrid search
isn't just one query.

00:12:10.320 --> 00:12:15.090
It basically is two searches
plus a ranking step on top,

00:12:15.090 --> 00:12:18.960
so what's happening here is
the first common expression is

00:12:18.960 --> 00:12:21.660
running a vector search,
grabbing the top 10 rows

00:12:21.660 --> 00:12:25.020
by meaning, and the second
common table expression is

00:12:25.020 --> 00:12:26.490
running a keyword search,

00:12:26.490 --> 00:12:29.250
grabbing the top 10
by exact word match.

00:12:29.250 --> 00:12:33.960
Then we're combining them using
something called reciprocal rank

00:12:33.960 --> 00:12:36.510
fusion or RRF.

00:12:36.510 --> 00:12:38.370
The data is super simple.

00:12:38.370 --> 00:12:43.350
Rows that rank high in
both lists basically win,

00:12:43.350 --> 00:12:46.680
so I'm asking what dietary
restrictions the user has.

00:12:46.680 --> 00:12:49.170
The vector is looking
for the meaning.

00:12:49.170 --> 00:12:52.290
Keyword is looking for the words
like "allergy" and "peanut,"

00:12:52.290 --> 00:12:55.620
and as you can see, the peanut
allergy row comes first

00:12:55.620 --> 00:13:00.000
because it's scored well in both
of these searches, the vector

00:13:00.000 --> 00:13:02.550
and the keyword search.

00:13:02.550 --> 00:13:06.030
One query, one database,
no separate engines

00:13:06.030 --> 00:13:08.410
to keep everything in sync.

00:13:10.440 --> 00:13:11.610
The last one is going

00:13:11.610 --> 00:13:14.980
to be asset transactions
across tables.

00:13:16.920 --> 00:13:21.060
So real agents, as we know, do
multi-step writes that have

00:13:21.060 --> 00:13:24.390
to land together, and
there are two tables here.

00:13:24.390 --> 00:13:26.760
So there's a memory table
you've been seeing,

00:13:26.760 --> 00:13:29.490
and there's also a
separate user facts table

00:13:29.490 --> 00:13:33.330
where we track aggregate stats
per user, like their trips

00:13:33.330 --> 00:13:35.760
and their memory count.

00:13:35.760 --> 00:13:38.610
Now, what's happening here is
I'm wrapping a transaction

00:13:38.610 --> 00:13:42.120
around two writes,
insert into memories,

00:13:42.120 --> 00:13:45.540
update the user facts
table, and then commit.

00:13:45.540 --> 00:13:48.450
As you can see, the new
memory is in the memories,

00:13:48.450 --> 00:13:51.630
and trip counters
went from 0 to 1

00:13:51.630 --> 00:13:55.230
and memory counter went
from 0 to 1 as well.

00:13:55.230 --> 00:13:58.770
Both writes landed together,
and if either one had failed,

00:13:58.770 --> 00:14:00.480
neither would have stuck.

00:14:00.480 --> 00:14:04.810
That's real distributed asset
across multiple tables.

00:14:07.470 --> 00:14:09.090
Okay, so that was
the whole demo.

00:14:09.090 --> 00:14:12.630
Now let me close out with a few
companies actually running TiDB

00:14:12.630 --> 00:14:14.040
in production.

00:14:14.040 --> 00:14:17.910
These are real numbers
and not just benchmarks.

00:14:17.910 --> 00:14:19.680
The first one is Manus.

00:14:19.680 --> 00:14:21.750
They are an AI startup company,

00:14:21.750 --> 00:14:24.750
and every agent gets
its own database spun

00:14:24.750 --> 00:14:26.850
up in milliseconds.

00:14:26.850 --> 00:14:29.580
Over a million and a half
of these agents right now,

00:14:29.580 --> 00:14:35.280
and they moved over to TiDB and
got to production in two weeks.

00:14:35.280 --> 00:14:36.600
Then we have Dify.

00:14:36.600 --> 00:14:40.770
They are an AI dev platform
company and their backend used

00:14:40.770 --> 00:14:46.710
to be a 500,000 containers, and
they moved all of it onto TiDB.

00:14:46.710 --> 00:14:51.000
Now they just have one
engine that does everything,

00:14:51.000 --> 00:14:52.710
and then we also have Pinterest.

00:14:52.710 --> 00:14:54.690
I mean, they are
not an AI company,

00:14:54.690 --> 00:14:57.210
but the problem they
had was very similar.

00:14:57.210 --> 00:15:00.690
They had six different databases
systems doing different jobs.

00:15:00.690 --> 00:15:05.070
Now they'll have only one, which
is TiDB, and TiDB is doing,

00:15:05.070 --> 00:15:10.000
basically, 1.3 million queries
per second for Pinterest.

00:15:11.070 --> 00:15:14.220
Across all three, the story
